## Simple example: a wrapper for an unmanaged file handle

```csharp
using System;
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using System.Text;

class NativeFile : IDisposable
{
    // Simulate an unmanaged handle (e.g., from Win32 CreateFile)
    private IntPtr _handle;
    private bool _disposed = false;   // track disposal state

    public NativeFile(string path)
    {
        // For illustration we use the Windows API CreateFile
        _handle = CreateFile(
            path,
            GENERIC_READ,
            FILE_SHARE_READ,
            IntPtr.Zero,
            OPEN_EXISTING,
            FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL,
            IntPtr.Zero);

        if (_handle == INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE)
            throw new System.ComponentModel.Win32Exception();
    }

    public string ReadAllText()
    {
        if (_disposed)
            throw new ObjectDisposedException(nameof(NativeFile));

        const int bufferSize = 4096;
        byte[] buffer = new byte[bufferSize];
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();

        int bytesRead;
        while ((bytesRead = ReadFile(_handle, buffer, bufferSize, out var bytesReadOut, IntPtr.Zero)) > 0)
        {
            sb.Append(Encoding.UTF8.GetString(buffer, 0, bytesRead));
        }

        return sb.ToString();
    }

    // ---------- IDisposable implementation ----------
    public void Dispose()
    {
        Dispose(true);
        GC.SuppressFinalize(this);   // we cleaned up manually
    }

    protected virtual void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        if (_disposed) return;

        // Free unmanaged resources
        if (_handle != IntPtr.Zero && _handle != INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE)
        {
            CloseHandle(_handle);
            _handle = IntPtr.Zero;
        }

        // If 'disposing' were true, we could also dispose managed objects here

        _disposed = true;
    }

    // ---------- Finalizer ----------
    ~NativeFile()
    {
        Dispose(false);   // only unmanaged cleanup
    }

    // ---------- P/Invoke helpers ----------
    private const uint GENERIC_READ = 0x80000000;
    private const uint FILE_SHARE_READ = 0x00000001;
    private const uint OPEN_EXISTING = 3;
    private const uint FILE_ATTRIBUTE_NORMAL = 0x80;
    private static readonly IntPtr INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE = new IntPtr(-1);

    [DllImport("kernel32.dll", SetLastError = true, CharSet = CharSet.Unicode)]
    private static extern IntPtr CreateFile(
        string lpFileName,
        uint dwDesiredAccess,
        uint dwShareMode,
        IntPtr lpSecurityAttributes,
        uint dwCreationDisposition,
        uint dwFlagsAndAttributes,
        IntPtr hTemplateFile);

    [DllImport("kernel32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
    private static extern bool CloseHandle(IntPtr hObject);

    [DllImport("kernel32.dll", SetLastError = true)]
    private static extern int ReadFile(
        IntPtr hFile,
        [Out] byte[] lpBuffer,
        int nNumberOfBytesToRead,
        out int lpNumberOfBytesRead,
        IntPtr lpOverlapped);
}
```

### Using it

```csharp
try
{
    using var file = new NativeFile(@"C:\temp\hello.txt");
    string contents = file.ReadAllText();
    Console.WriteLine(contents);
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Error: {ex.Message}");
}
```

When the `using` block exits (normally or because of an exception), `Dispose()` is called automatically, which in turn:

1. Calls `CloseHandle(_handle)` to release the native file handle.
2. Marks the object as disposed so that subsequent calls to `ReadAllText()` throw an `ObjectDisposedException`.
3. Suppresses finalization so the finalizer won’t run again.

If for some reason you forget to wrap the object in `using`, the finalizer (`~NativeFile`) will still run when the GC collects the object, ensuring the unmanaged resource is eventually freed. But you’ll lose the deterministic guarantee.

---

## Key take‑aways

| Concept | What it is | When to use |
|---------|------------|-------------|
| `IDisposable` | Interface with `Dispose()` method | Implement on any type that owns unmanaged resources or large managed resources that should be released early. |
| `using` block | Compiler‑generated `try/finally` that calls `Dispose()` | Whenever you create a disposable object and you know you’re done with it after some code runs. |
| Finalizer (`~ClassName`) | Runtime‑invoked cleanup as a safety net | Use only as a fallback; it runs non‑deterministically and may keep the object alive for a while. |
| `GC.SuppressFinalize(this)` | Tells GC that finalizer is no longer needed | Call inside `Dispose()` after you’ve cleaned up manually. |

In practice, most of the time you’ll just:

```csharp
using (var resource = new SomeDisposableType())
{
    // use resource
}
```

and that’s all you need to know. The runtime will do the rest.
